## Why MIT

This project is documentation, schemas, a small validator, and a static dashboard —
developer tooling meant to be read, reused, and adapted freely by anyone integrating
with the GLOBE API, including by NASA/GLOBE's own documentation team if they find it
useful. MIT was chosen over:

- **A copyleft license (e.g. GPL)**, because this project has no interest in requiring
  downstream projects that merely *use* these schemas or this validator to also
  open-source their own code. Imposing that would reduce, not increase, how useful this
  is to other developers.
- **Apache-2.0**, which would add an explicit patent grant — unnecessary here, since
  this project contains no patentable techniques, only documentation, JSON Schemas, and
  straightforward validation/display code.
- **A no-license / all-rights-reserved default**, which would actually work against the
  stated goal (a reusable developer reference) by leaving reuse rights ambiguous.

MIT is short, widely understood, and places no burden on anyone who wants to copy a
schema file into their own project or fork the dashboard.
